WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:26.520
McDonalds is everywhere.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:31.119
In the contiguous United States, the farthest
you can get from one is this spot in the Sheldon

00:00:31.119 --> 00:00:33.700
National Antelope Refuge in Nevada.

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:38.820
Even then, you only have to drive 115 miles
to the nearest golden arches.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:45.210
Around there world, there are more than 36,000
locations in 119 different countries.

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:50.920
One of the defining characteristics of McDonald’s
is the commitment to consistency across the

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:51.920
brand.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:54.989
While there are regional differences to the
menu such as KiwiBurgers in New Zealand or

00:00:54.989 --> 00:00:59.920
Chicken Congee in Indonesia, the core menu
items such as Big Macs, Chicken Nuggets, and

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:02.579
Fries are always avaliable.

00:01:02.579 --> 00:01:06.610
That means that the same items are being made
in the same way with the same ingredients

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:10.380
in very different economies around the world.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:12.960
Obviously, BigMacs don’t cost the same everywhere.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:18.220
A Big Mac costs on average $4.79 in the US,
so if you were to charge the equivalent in

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:23.670
Kenya, 480 Shilling, nobody would buy it because
that’s double the average daily salary.

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:27.500
That’d be like charging $200 for a big mac
in the US.

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:31.740
That’s why McDonald’s sets the local price
for burgers based on a variety of factors

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:36.100
such as labor costs, rent, ingredient costs,
and local income levels.

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:38.270
Now for some real economics.

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:42.310
Purchasing Power is the amount of goods you
can get for one unit of currency.

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:43.600
Let me explain.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:48.380
In the US, the average price of a Big Mac
across all markets is $4.79.

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:56.049
In Sweden, the cost of a BigMac is 44 krona.
44 Krona is equivalent to $5.13.

00:01:56.049 --> 00:02:01.189
That means that in the US, you can get 20.8%
of a BigMac for a dollar, while in Sweden,

00:02:01.189 --> 00:02:04.150
you can only get 19.5% of that burger.

00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:08.950
So, in terms of the BigMac, the purchasing
power of the US dollar is higher.

00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:10.970
You get more bang for your buck.

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:15.310
This also means that the Swedish Krona is
overvalued in terms of exchange rates by about

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:18.950
6.1%, at least according to the Big Mac Index.

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:25.160
The Economist Magazine first created the index
in 1986 as an informal way of comparing Purchasing

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:27.320
Power between currencies.

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:29.150
So now let’s look at the numbers.

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:34.730
In Venezuela, the cost of a BigMac is equivalent
to $0.66—that’s the cheapest Big Mac in

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:37.680
terms of equivalent US dollars in the world.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:42.390
This is in largely due to recent currency
devaluations and high inflation in the country.

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:47.200
It’s possible that the cost of a Venezuelan
Big Mac will soon rise in response.

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:52.629
This means that for the cost of one American
Big Mac, you could buy 7 Venezuelan Big Macs.

00:02:52.629 --> 00:02:58.019
After Venezuela, the countries with the cheapest
Big Macs are Russia, Ukraine, South Africa,

00:02:58.019 --> 00:02:59.069
and Malaysia.

00:02:59.069 --> 00:03:04.019
For the other side of the spectrum, the top
five most expensive big macs come from Switzerland,

00:03:04.019 --> 00:03:07.459
Sweden, Norway, the US, then Denmark.

00:03:07.459 --> 00:03:12.360
That’s right, the US is home to the fourth
most expensive Big Mac in the world.

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:16.849
What’s also interesting is to look at how
long it takes individuals to earn enough money

00:03:16.849 --> 00:03:20.420
to buy a BigMac in different cities around
the world.

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:25.569
In Nairobi, Kenya, an average worker would
have to rack up 173 minutes on the clock to

00:03:25.569 --> 00:03:27.370
pay for one Big Mac.

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:32.409
Compare that to an worker in Hong Kong, who
would only have to work 8.7 minutes for a

00:03:32.409 --> 00:03:33.409
burger.

00:03:33.409 --> 00:03:36.970
The quickest one can earn a burger in the
US is in Miami, where the average individual

00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:40.909
would only have to work for 10.7 minutes to
pay for a Big Mac.

00:03:40.909 --> 00:03:42.960
Now, this index isn’t perfect.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:47.040
While the cost of ingredients is pretty similar
across all countries since all ingredients

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:51.340
are held to the same standard, the index doesn’t
account for the wide differences in labor

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:53.069
and land costs.

00:03:53.069 --> 00:03:57.280
A burger will cost less to make in India because
McDonald’s doesn’t need to pay as much

00:03:57.280 --> 00:03:59.730
for employees and retail space.

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:05.879
Nevertheless, this index is a great way of
teaching some basic economics in a tasty way.

